From abaacfff9efda7aeddbe2216c3a90125f94136ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: whn09 <wanghenan09@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 13:59:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 07/10] vdn: convert RGB to YUV420p on the GPU instead of in
 swscale

With the video VAE spread over the ranks the mp4 mux became the largest single item in a
480p render: 2.65 s, against 2.11 s for the whole eight-way decode, all of it on the host.

scripts/mux_bench.py decomposes it, and the answer was not the one the shape of the problem
suggests. It is not x264:

    VideoFrame.from_ndarray(rgb24)      0.30 s
    reformat rgb24 -> yuv420p           2.03 s   <-- swscale, single-threaded
    x264 encode, thread_count=0         1.72 s   (already threaded; 6.40 s at 1)

x264 is already using the box. What is not is swscale's colour conversion, which runs
serially in the calling thread -- which is why setting thread_count on the stream buys only
1.17x. The pixels are on the GPU as float32 in [0, 1] and the conversion is a 3x3 matrix and
a 2x2 average, so it is done there instead, and it also halves the host transfer: yuv420p is
1.5 bytes/px against rgb24's 3, so 345 frames of 864x480 cross PCIe as 215 MB, not 430.

This re-implements a lossy conversion rather than refactoring one, so the encoded bytes
change and it sits behind render.gpu_color_convert (default true, false is the old path
exactly). Equivalence is measured, not assumed -- `mux_bench.py --verify` compares against
swscale's own output on real frames:

    Y: max abs error 1   PSNR 70.96 dB      U: 6, 55.10 dB      V: 6, 56.90 dB

Y is checked on max absolute error because that is where a wrong matrix (BT.709) or a wrong
range (full) would show, and both would be tens of levels. Chroma is checked on PSNR: the
2x2 box average is the closest of the three plausible filters to swscale (54.8 dB against
53.9 for taking the top row), so the residual is swscale's fixed-point arithmetic against
this file's float, and it is ours that is the more accurate. It is also far below x264's
chroma quantiser step at the default CRF, so it cannot reach the file.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
